    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`, and both tools work when called over stdio.\n\n**About the pinned version:** `mcp==2.2.0` has a different API from the 1.x SDK. The high-level decorator class is now `MCPServer` (from `mcp.server.mcpserver`), replacing `FastMCP`. How errors reach the model also changed. In 2.x, if a tool raises an ordinary exception, the model sees only \"Error executing tool <name>\" and not your message. So `word_count` raises `ToolError` (from `mcp.server.mcpserver.exceptions`) for an unknown title. That makes the call come back as a tool error with the \"no such note\" message included.\n\n**What's in the file:**\n- The server is `MCPServer(\"notes\")`, with `add_note` and `word_count` registered using `@server.tool()`.\n- `add_note` saves the note in `NOTES` and returns `\"saved <title>\"`.\n- `word_count` returns `len(NOTES[title].split())`, so any run of whitespace counts as one separator.\n- `python -m app.server` still calls `build_server().run()`, which serves over stdio by default.\n\n**Checks I ran:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed.\n- `python -m app.smoke`: lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n- A test client talking to the server over stdio:\n  - `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` returned `saved a`.\n  - `word_count(\"a\")` returned `4`.\n  - `word_count(\"zz\")` came back as a tool error with the message `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: zz`.\n\nI didn't add any dependencies. I couldn't delete the temporary test script `e2e_check.py` from the project root because the `rm` command needed approval. Please delete it yourself."
  },
